From 00fefac0b3ac3507f48e4493866dd4a54d6395f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MiaAI-Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:35:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/13] glm cuda: a prompt pass's EXL3 experts in one call,
 written into the MoE slot rows

GLM ran the common EXL3 prompt kernels in calls of 1024 rows (cuda_exl3.chunk_rows), so
every expert's trellis was decoded twice a 2048-row pass for about 28 pairs an expert. A
call now takes the whole pass: chunk_rows follows cuda_state.prefill_rows. A pair's sums do
not depend on which item of 64 holds it, so the bits are the same.

The down kernel writes the routed slots of the block's slot rows itself: a new last argument
yrow (0: y [pairs][dims] as before) places pair p = row * ys + slot at row * yrow + slot, and
GLM passes ey with yrow = top_k + 1. That drops the cuMemcpy2D of [rows][8][4096] fp32 after
every call and the prompt arena's y (exl3_prompt.bytesFor / scratchFor with own_y false).
The grouped (runSlots) path keeps its copy. Registers and shared memory of
tf_exl3_prompt_down/_k6 are unchanged on sm_120 (127/43264, 128/40192).

Measured on an RTX PRO 6000 (sm_120, 400 W): routed experts per 2048 rows 4.84 -> 3.50 ms
(-28%) with skewed routing; one 2048-row call writing the slot rows is byte-identical to two
1024-row calls and the copy (3/4-, 4/4- and 3/3-bit layers); exl3-prompt-k6 passes. In the
engine, with the next two changes, cold prefill 4K +12.6%, 16K +15.6%, 64K +16.0%, T=0
replies unchanged.

